One common way the Java API can be misused is through improper handling of user input when interacting with the operating system, such as using `Runtime.getRuntime().exec()`. This can lead to Command Injection vulnerabilities.

```java
import java.io.*;

public class CommandExecutor {
    public void runSystemCommand(String userInput) throws IOException {
        // This method is vulnerable to Command Injection because it concatenates 
        // unvalidated user input directly into a shell command string.
        // An attacker could input "ls; rm -rf /" to execute malicious commands.
        String command = "ping -c 1 " + userInput;
        Runtime.getRuntime().exec(command);
    }
}
```
***
```java
import java.io.*;
import java.util.List;

public class CommandExecutor {
    public void runSystemCommand(String userInput) throws IOException {
        // This version is secure because it uses a ProcessBuilder with a List of arguments.
        // The operating system treats the entire userInput as a single argument to the 'ping' command,
        // preventing the shell from interpreting meta-characters like ';' or '&'.
        ProcessBuilder pb = new ProcessBuilder("ping", "-c", "1", userInput);
        pb.start();
    }
}
```

